A typical argument for subsidies in various sectors (especially agriculture) is that other countries also subsidize "domestic" producers, so we have to keep ours competitive through subsidies; it looks beautiful at first glance, but from an economic point of view we can't help ourselves (
I discussed the reasons earlier
). But now I'm interested in another thing: Why should I help some farmer, whom I don't even know, to be competitive with another farmer, whom I also don't know, just because the first one is Czech? I care about the welfare of people (regardless of state); and I care more about close people than less close ones, about acquaintances more than about strangers, it seems natural and self-evident to me. But it is not clear to me why the welfare of the Czechs should be more important to me than the welfare of any other people; for what reason should I feel a sense of belonging towards someone just based on how some politicians have drawn some boundary lines on the map?

Do you know the difference between a minarchist (proponent of a small state) and an anarcho-capitalist? Three months. No, seriously... although many anarcho-capitalists define themselves against minarchists as actually being statists (and from the point of view of us anarchists, of course they are), I like minarchists. On the one hand, minarchism may not even be inconsistent (the individual may be in favor of maximizing respect for property rights and at the same time believe that the maximum that can realistically be achieved lies in a small state), but above all, the state is so large today that I am absolutely convinced that people who want to reduce it should pull together; however tempting it may be for someone to define himself against the other group and thereby show his opinion, of which he is appropriately proud, it will not help anything. For fights over whether taxes should be low or none (
not that there isn't a fundamental difference between the two
), there will be plenty of time before we manage to ensure that they are at least not huge.

Many free-spirited people get a blindfold in their pockets when they hear of unions; and it's totally understandable... in this day and age. But it was not always so; unions were originally created in a free market environment as a (quite legitimate) association of employees for collective bargaining with the employer - instead of employees going to the employer to negotiate one by one, they joined together and did it all at once to improve their position. Such efforts are perfectly fine, like strikes, if they take place in a market environment where the employer can send the unions to hell and respond to the strike by hiring new workers, which is fine;
however, the state brings the problem into the whole thing
(how else). At a time when the (perhaps primary) mission of unions is to pressure employers through legislation, i.e. using coercion, it is more or less a criminal organization; although many members do not realize this and "mean well".

What about the laws of the market? It depends on how we define them; and since mainstream economics often defines market laws (of price, supply and demand, competition, and so on) in an environment of perfect elasticity, information, competition, and the like, one could say that they never apply. However, Austrian economics formulates market laws with regard to reality, i.e. without those unnecessary unrealistic assumptions; however, people - accustomed to mainstream economics - mostly do not know anything about the Austrian school of economics, so they like to talk about "exceptions" to the laws of supply and demand in cases where the market works significantly differently than in ideal macroeconomic models. However, that doesn't have to be a bad thing! Even an industry where there is practically only one entity that eliminates all competition does not have to be "non-market" or bad for customers (
see Standard Oil
), unless it is caused by state intervention (the only ones that really distort markets).

People typically don't like politicians very much; or at least most of them. They know them to be corrupt, they make fun of them at best, they despise them at worst; yes, sometimes they have their favourites, but few are satisfied with the majority of those who rule and make laws. People would hardly go to politicians for advice on ethical issues in their lives; and I totally understand, I wouldn't go to them for advice either. But then why are the laws so sacred for everyone? After all, laws are only what are voted by the very people whose moral credit and abilities most of us doubt; and it cannot be logically consistent to say that a politician is not the one who could give advice, but at the same time it is right to follow his will. But this is not about logic, but about emotions;
it was pushed into our heads by state propaganda
, that the laws are right and we must respect them - even when it's obvious they don't make sense.

Politicians like to stir up "class conflicts": poor versus rich, customers versus businessmen, employees versus employers, natives versus foreigners, and various other "us" versus "them" groups; for politicians, such conflicts are good because they feed them - they can express themselves about them, they can "solve" them, they can join different sides, they can get votes from them. People then forget how much and often these groups intermingle and how strongly they are related to each other (
for example, the worse the position of employers, the fewer there will be; they will become employees in the labor market, which will worsen their position due to higher supply - the law of supply and demand applies here as well
); above all, however, they forget that the real common enemy of all decent people is not other decent people, but the state, which pits us against each other through unjust laws, from which it then benefits, as confused people call for a "solution" in the form of more bad laws.

In debates about a free society, it is quite common practice for statists to demand from free market advocates a defense of why a free market society should work better than a state-run one; although I typically approach this debate and try to provide such justifications, this is actually a very absurd situation. Why should he who wants to keep everyone what he earns defend his opinion against one who proposes aggression to rob those funds? In order for everyone to keep what is his, no fundamental defenses are needed; on the contrary, the use of violence to obtain funds from others is something that, in my opinion, cannot even be defended, but if someone should try to do it, he should definitely have exceptionally strong arguments! Just because the status quo is currently perverted (historically as a result of various "by the will of God the king" arguments), those debates are somewhat reversed;
non-aggression must be defended
.

Interest arose quite naturally long before economists were able to explain its nature, with the result that it was often condemned on absurd grounds; and unfortunately even today many consider them immoral or undesirable. So why do they arise? Firstly, a loan is always associated with risk (even with the best will of the borrower, he may not be able to repay the debt) and secondly, we value "goods now" more than "goods later"; I deliberately write about property in general, not only about money, because interest is not a monetary phenomenon, but a completely general one - the creditor gives up something and the interest makes a fee that compensates him for not having it (and the risk of losing it). Monetary interest is sometimes referred to as "irredeemable" within the economy as a whole without creating new money; however, this is a big mistake, because one crown can be used to repay the same debt more than once (when the creditor spends it and the debtor gets it);
therefore, there is nothing immoral or undesirable about interest
.

Politicians make us feel that we should be interested in politics and "be active"; well of course they want us to care about them. Knowing who holds which ministerial post, what it looks like and what it is, is usually perceived positively (sometimes someone shows me appreciation, for example, for the fact that I know the structure of the state budget by heart and have some overview of politicians - mainly because of how often I criticize them); on the contrary, those who are not interested in politics sometimes tend to apologize for it. But I don't think they have anything to be ashamed of; and on the contrary, I am not at all proud of rummaging through the cesspool so often, it is no virtue. In general, "being active in public life" is not inherently good; one can be "publicly active" both positively and negatively. And I'm actually happy for the one who doesn't go to the elections, only cares about himself and doesn't even know who the president, prime minister and finance minister are;
if everyone was like that, we would have peace of mind
.

A huge number of people hate capitalism; or at least she doesn't like him very much. However, it is not entirely clear to me what they don't really like... that everyone decides what they will do, what they don't want to do, what they will buy and not buy, what services they will and will not provide, what agreements they will make, who they will employ, not employ and under what conditions? Do they not like that people have the freedom to live their lives as they see fit and desire to force them to submit to the will of the powerful (mostly the state)? Do they want to rule the lives of others just for fun? Would it suit them that we live in misery and want caused by the impossibility of economic calculation in central planning? No. I don't believe those people are evil (at least most of the time). They don't want us to suffer. Unfortunately, this suffering is linked to capitalism; usually without any deeper justification or logical analysis. The word itself has a huge negative connotation;
let's clean them up
!

Although almost everyone acknowledges that wars are terrible, many people see their "bright side" in that they bring technological and economic development, and post-war reparations give people jobs; why are these so-called "advantages" illusory rather than real? Well, if you come up to someone, smash their car, causing them to start working overtime to save up for a new one, you may have "stimulated the economy", but it's hard to call that a positive thing; the same thing happens in war, only on a mass scale. Faster technological development is indeed taking place, but since there is no such thing as a free lunch, it comes at the expense of something; and what? The state will take resources from the people to finance war research; they then cannot buy what they prefer. For if they themselves desired technological development at the expense of other goods, they certainly would not need war to invest in science on a voluntary basis;
if they don't, it's not worth it to them
.

"People are not yet advanced enough not to need someone to govern them," say many when they criticize anarchy; and some commoner nods knowingly when he thinks of all those stupid people (
and it's not really that hot either, as I discussed in one lecture
). But how can the same people, who are no longer mature enough to take responsibility for their own lives, be able to responsibly choose their rulers? At the same time, the difficulty of the task "to take care of yourself, pay the bills, somehow live with money and think at least a little about the future" is, after all, an order of magnitude lower than the difficulty of the task "to guess the consequences of your democratic choice", because it includes, on the one hand, an estimate of whether the elected candidate is lying, but above all an assessment of the effects of his political program from the point of view of the economy and many other aspects, all of which are extremely complex and involve not only the voter himself, but the entire society. Do you see the contradiction?

Very often we come across the argument that although redistribution hinders the overall enrichment of society brought about by capitalism, we must "find a balance" between economic growth and social justice, because we are obliged to take care of the poor even at the expense of our own prosperity; but do the promoters of such theories realize all the implications? In particular, let's compare the quality of, for example, education, health care, housing or food of the poorest members of society now and a hundred years ago; huge difference, huh? And what if we slow down this development by redistribution? Although it might help someone (and it is
debatable
), but at the expense of all those who will have to suffer or die at any later time just because the technology that would solve their problems was not yet available or widespread (due to the slowness of development); every choice carries with it an opportunity cost that cannot be neglected.

What do you think of that headline? Fits the libertarian tabloid that Stoky basically is, doesn't it? Nice to blow it up so everyone can see how awful the state is. However, I'm not very good at inventing such headlines - I took this one from the official website of the Police of the Czech Republic (I won't link it, but it's easy to google it); the uniformed monkeys even brag about it, that for six flowers in flower pots outside the window, one goes to grumble. The piquancy, which does not follow from the title (but is mentioned in the article), reads as follows: "According to the established facts, he intended to harvest these plants, dry them and further process them into the target drug marijuana. He then wanted to use this narcotic and psychotropic substance for his own needs and for the production of cannabis ointments." So no one even expected it to be a dealer; this is purely about helping and protecting yourself. Not that it would be okay if it was a dealer or some other drug;
the whole "war on drugs" thing is bullshit anyway
.

Subway places around the world accept Bitcoin; in Brno they now also accept Litecoin! They thus become the first Subway branches in the world where you can pay not only with Bitcoin, but also with Litecoin. This is due to high bitcoin transaction fees and a possible network fork in August; regardless, it's great that another cryptocurrency is being brought into the mainstream. I have to admit that I am pleasantly surprised; I expected that cryptocurrencies would spread, but probably not the speed with which it is happening (fortunately, I bought in time; and I can happily continue to buy). This fascinating rate of growth has another side effect: Quite possibly the states (as a result of their
desperate inefficiencies
) simply do not have time to react; a "cryptocurrency = child porn" campaign doesn't work so well when people pay with it on every corner and people know about it. Unfortunately, we are not that far yet; but will we be before the states recover?

A certain vegan Erwin Kessler lived in Switzerland; he disliked the Jewish rituals of animal sacrifice and compared them to the Holocaust (not that I agree with him, I am based on other axioms, but I appreciate his logical consistency). The court sent him to sweatpants for that (because
freedom of speech
). A few years later, Kessler was called out by a few people on social media for being anti-Semitic and racist. Kessler didn't understand his life lesson about freedom of speech and sued the person in question. And guess what? Not only did he succeed (although the defendants scolded him for what the state put him behind bars for), but even someone who just "liked" the post was convicted! God's precedent, right? Normally I'd say it would be laughable if it wasn't a cry; however, this is so absurd that it makes you laugh and cry at the same time: A vegan gets stitched up for a "wrong" opinion; he will not understand where the problem is; they punish anti-vegans for having the "correct" opinion; and some passerby for kneeling xD

I don't like Alza;
they were extremely rude to me there
. What do I do with it? Well, I just don't shop there; problem solved. Not so ČOIka - even Alza didn't do anything to them, yet she goes there to pout; why? Because they are open on holidays; what do they afford? The seller and his staff want to sell goods voluntarily, people want to buy, a win-win deal; and the politician will say that they are simply unlucky, nothing will happen! The Czech Trade Inspection then runs there like a state pooch, "just doing its job" and sues Alza for mega; moreover, this is not the first case in December, the exact same thing happened in October. If this e-shop is going to continue to do this and programmatically ignore the law, maybe my antipathy towards them will disappear and I will start buying from them just to support them; and if they are somewhat rebellious and non-conformist, I will probably visit there for my next purchase.

Ninety-year-old Arnold Abbott in Florida spends his time in an interesting way; feeding the homeless alternates cyclically with a stay in prison. Why? According to local laws, homeless people can only be fed by non-profit organizations chosen by the city (which looks like decent business) and only if they bring a mobile toilet with them; however, Mr. Abbott does not meet these conditions, ergo he has to pay fines and go to jail for handing out food. In the last action of the heroic uniformed monkeys, the line was even heard: "Drop the plate right now!" followed by the intervention of the lawmen, who dumped all the undistributed food in the garbage right in front of the hungry; in connection with this, one cannot help but think of state propaganda using poor and powerless people as a pretext and justification for the existence of the state - while it is precisely for such people that the state is (
typically for other reasons
) most malignant.
